  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "To pass on news, say who spoke and add **nti** (that): **Agamba nti…**, he says that…",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Owulidde amawulire?", "base": "Have you heard the news?" },
      { "target": "Agamba nti enkuba ejja.", "base": "He says that rain is coming." },
      { "target": "Bagamba nti ekkubo eriggya liwedde.", "base": "They say that the new road is finished." },
      { "target": "Ndowooza nti kirungi.", "base": "I think that it is good." },
      { "target": "Endowooza yo ki?", "base": "What is your opinion?" },
      { "target": "Nzikiriza naawe.", "base": "I agree with you." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "reported-speech",
        "title": "Saying what others said",
        "body": [
          "**nti** works like English “that”. Put it after a verb of saying or thinking, then give the words: **Agamba nti…** (he says that…), **Bagamba nti…** (they say / people say that…), **Ndowooza nti…** (I think that…).",
          "The words after **nti** keep their own tense, so **Agamba nti ajja** is “he says that he is coming”."
        ],
        "table": [["Luganda", "English"], ["Ngamba nti…", "I say that…"], ["Agamba nti…", "He / she says that…"], ["Bagamba nti…", "They say that…"]]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Radio and the village meeting",
        "body": [
          "Radio is still the main way news travels in Buganda, with lively call-in shows in Luganda. Local decisions are made at the **olukiiko**, where the **ssentebe** lets everyone give their **endowooza**."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
